The speed of the train is 45 km/hr, which is:
\[ 45 \text{ km/hr} = 45 \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{3600 \text{ s}} = \frac{45000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} \]

The train is moving at a speed of 12.5 m/s, and the jogger is moving at a speed of 2.5 m/s. Since they are moving in the same direction, the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger is the difference between their speeds:
\[ 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 2.5 \text{ m/s} = 10 \text{ m/s} \]

The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. This means that the train covers the distance it has to to reach the jogger and then also covers the length of the train to pass the jogger. The initial distance of the jogger to the entrance of the train is 150 meters.

The time it takes for the train to reach the starting point of the jogger is:
\[ \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative speed}} = \frac{150 \text{ m}}{10 \text{ m/s}} = 15 \text{ seconds} \]

During this time, the train travels 150 meters relative to the jogger.

