\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 250 \text{ meters} \]

The distance covered by the train to pass the jogger is the sum of the initial distance of the jogger to the edge of the train and the length of the train. So, we can set up the equation:
\[ 150 \text{ m} + \text{Length of the train} = 250 \text{ meters} \]

Solving for the length of the train:
\[ \text{Length of the train} = 250 \text{ meters} - 150 \text{ meters} = 100 \text{ meters} \]

